/// Two units may export the same name.
///
/// Refused outright today.  The interpreter says ''Circle'' is already defined;
/// mark it private in one of the modules.' and the compiler says 'Two modules
/// exporting ''Circle'' is not supported by the C back end yet.'
///
/// ⚠️ The interpreter's advice is the tell.  'Mark it private in one of the
/// modules' asks the programmer to AVOID the collision rather than resolve it,
/// which is only advice a language gives when it has no way to say which name
/// is meant.  Qualification is that way, and this is what it is for: a module
/// system whose names must be globally unique has not separated anything.
///
/// A bare use of an ambiguous name stays an error, reported where it is used
/// rather than where the units are imported -- so importing two units that
/// happen to share a name costs nothing until a program writes the name.  That
/// case cannot be reproduced here, being a refusal.
///
/// ⚠️ Ordered behind #17.  This file's qualified TYPE annotations are refused
/// before the duplicate export is ever reached, so it currently reports
/// 'Expect '';'' after variable declaration.'  Strip the annotations and the
/// real message appears: ''Circle'' is already defined; mark it private in one
/// of the modules.'  Qualified type names therefore have to land first, or this
/// reproduction cannot even reach the defect it is for.
///
/// Fails with: Expect ';' after variable declaration.  (file rejected -- no test ran)
///
uses 'lib/Round';
uses 'lib/Flat';

test 'Two units may export the same name';
begin
    var Curved : Round.Circle := Round.Circle ();
    var Plane  : Flat.Circle  := Flat.Circle ();

    AssertEqual (Str ('Round'), Curved.Kind ());
    AssertEqual (Str ('Flat'),  Plane.Kind ());
    AssertTrue  (Curved is Round.Circle);
    AssertTrue  (Plane  is Flat.Circle);
end
